    limitation: 'A bounded Timor-Leste political history from the 1974 decolonization opening through the 2023 parliamentary election, not a current-country claim or a score for Timorese, Tetum, Portuguese or other language communities, municipalities, customary authorities, churches, parties, resistance networks, veterans, women, youth, diaspora, voters, the President, Government, Parliament, courts, CNE, Indonesia, Portugal, Australia, the United Nations or any later government. The Constitution, UN documents and election records establish formal texts, diplomatic arrangements, a certified 1999 result, administrative sequences and provisional 2023 tabulation; none by itself proves uniform rights implementation, public consent, electoral fairness, judicial independence, civilian control, local authority, gender equality, reconciliation, accountability, maritime settlement, economic distribution or legitimacy. Molnar, Kammen, Fox–Babo Soares, Pereira–Feijó, Roque and Feijó cover different periods, genres, methods and standpoints rather than a single scholarly consensus. Timorese-language and Indonesian-language scholarship, oral histories, women’s, youth, disability and local political histories, customary law and land records, municipal governance, church and resistance archives, occupation and justice documentation, maritime and petroleum records, independent election observation, and developments after 2023 remain open research tasks; no later current status is asserted.',
