    claim: 'The 1977 Constitution and 1992 amendment record establish the Union’s formal institutional and multiparty framework, while the 2015 and 2020 National Electoral Commission records anchor the selected electoral sequence. Kilambo and Miti provide the principal book-length study of Magufuli’s political and economic context, anti-corruption, industrialization, mining, debt and international pressure; Coulson supplies an independent political-economy history connecting the postcolonial state, Ujamaa, crisis and market/resource-sector reform; Tripp adds a social and urban account of liberalization and informal political-economic actors. Taken together, the sources support a changing and mixed six-axis interpretation: state-led developmental and revenue-seeking policy within market, mining and debt relations rather than a simple socialist or laissez-faire order; formal social and constitutional language alongside contested inclusion and public-order constraints; CCM and executive authority operating through Union, Parliament, courts and electoral bodies rather than a single institution; Tanzanian Union nationalism and sovereignty alongside mainland–Zanzibar distinctions and social, regional, religious and local variation; regional and international relations shaped by East African and southern African settings, mining, debt, development and the nonalignment legacy; and a formally secular constitutional order in which Christian, Muslim and customary authorities matter socially and politically without a clerical monopoly. The March 2021 succession is an endpoint of the selected case, not evidence for the whole subsequent Samia period. These are dated institutional and scholarly interpretations, not numerical scores or claims that Magufuli, CCM, Zanzibar, any religion, region, party or community represents Tanzania as a whole.',
