    limitation: 'Official constitutional, transitional, political-address and electoral records plus five book-length scholarly perspectives and a primary speech collection document formal designs, selected political practice and competing interpretations; they do not prove uniform implementation of rights, social programmes, constitutional limits, judicial independence, electoral fairness, civilian control, gender equality, local authority, public consent, security policy or the experience of every Burkinabè. The 2024 end is a dated transition and constitutional-review horizon, not a current-status claim; the later transition trajectory, security and displacement evidence, local and customary governance, women’s, labor, youth and urban–rural histories, local-language scholarship, opposition and armed-group sources, independent election observation, French and Burkinabè archival collections, and reception of the revised Charter remain open research tasks. The case is not a score for Burkina Faso, Burkinabè people, Mossi, Bobo, Fulani, Gurma, Tuareg, other ethnic, linguistic, religious, regional, gender, class or party communities, the CNR, the military, the presidency, armed groups or any current government.',
