    claim: 'The 2015 constitutional revision and the 2017 election establish a formal succession framework and an electoral endpoint for the post-genocide order, while the 2024 National Electoral Commission record documents the later presidential and legislative returns. Reyntjens’s 2024 political history supplies a critical longue-durée interpretation of post-genocide governance, militarisation, patronage and presidential power; Folarin’s fieldwork-informed book emphasizes state-led reconstruction, social programmes, resilience, national security and regional influence; Bachmann’s archival history adds a distinct historiographical account of post-genocide justice and reconstruction; Thomson’s earlier study keeps ordinary citizens, reconciliation and the precariousness of peace in view. The regional observation record adds a procedural perspective but does not replace domestic records or political opposition archives. On the six-axis framework, the bounded evidence supports a changing and mixed interpretation: state-coordinated developmental reconstruction, market participation and donor/international dependence rather than a simple socialist or laissez-faire model; formal equality, gender and rights commitments alongside national-unity, security, memory and social-control practices; a highly centralized executive-party state operating through constitutional institutions, elections and courts; a post-genocide national-unity project that officially rejects ethnic political mobilization while memory, return, regional and social experiences remain contested; regional security, refugee and Congo-linked politics alongside international partnerships and criticism; and a formally secular constitutional order with religious freedom rather than clerical government. The 2024 official record lists Kagame with 99.18 percent of the presidential vote and two other presidential candidates, but neither the returns nor observation record proves equal competition, public consent or the character of every Rwandan political current. These are dated institutional and scholarly interpretations, not numerical scores or claims that Kagame, the RPF, survivors, returnees, Hutu, Tutsi, Twa, any church, region or community represents Rwanda as a whole.',
    limitation: 'Constitutional, electoral and observation records plus book-length studies document formal design, selected institutional practice and competing interpretations; they do not prove uniform implementation of rights, judicial independence, electoral fairness, media freedom, civilian control, reconciliation, gender equality, land justice, socioeconomic distribution, public consent, local autonomy or the lived experience of every Rwandan. The 2024 end is a research horizon, not a current-status claim; Kinyarwanda and French primary and scholarly records, local and provincial histories, survivor and returnee testimony, Hutu, Tutsi and Twa perspectives, women’s, labor, youth and religious histories, opposition and media archives, development and land evidence, Rwanda–DRC/M23 sources, independent observation beyond the selected record and post-2024 politics remain open research tasks. The case is not a score for Rwanda, Rwandans, any ethnic, linguistic, religious, regional, class or party community, the RPF, the military, the presidency or any current government.',
