    claim: 'Iliffe supplies a long history of Tanganyika’s social and political formation through independence, while Pratt interprets the 1945–1968 critical phase and the emergence of Nyerere’s socialist strategy. Coulson connects colonial inheritance, Nyerere’s Ujamaa and self-reliance, one-party rule, economic crisis and later reform; Tripp adds a book-length account of liberalization, urban informal politics and the changing relationship between state regulation and social actors. Nyerere’s Ujamaa essays and the Arusha Declaration anchor the primary language of African socialism, self-reliance and social ownership; the Articles of Union and 1977 Constitution anchor the Union’s formal architecture; Parliament’s record identifies the 1992 restoration of multiparty politics and the 1995 return to multiparty elections; the National Electoral Commission’s 2015 report marks the selected contemporary horizon. On the six-axis framework, the bounded evidence supports a changing and mixed profile: African-socialist and later liberalizing political economy; egalitarian, educational and social-development aims alongside coercive villagization, uneven provision and contested inclusion; TANU/CCM party-state and executive authority within a Union structure, followed by formal multiparty competition; anti-colonial sovereignty, Swahili-mediated national integration and Union identity alongside Tanganyikan–Zanzibari constitutional distinctions; nonalignment, Pan-Africanism and support for liberation movements; and a secular constitutional state with religious plurality rather than clerical rule. These are dated institutional and scholarly interpretations, not numerical scores or claims that Nyerere, CCM, Zanzibar, any religion, region, party or community represents Tanzania as a whole.',
    limitation: 'The Articles of Union, constitutional texts, official parliamentary and electoral records, Nyerere’s writings and book records document formal designs, political arguments and selected historical interpretations; they do not prove uniform implementation of rights, electoral fairness, judicial independence, local autonomy, economic equality, gender equality, religious freedom, mainland–Zanzibar consent, or the lived experience of every rural and urban community. The 2015 end is a research horizon, not a current-status claim; Swahili-language and Zanzibar scholarship, Union negotiations, party congress records, constitutional debates, villagization and rural histories, women’s and labor histories, opposition records, regional inequality, religious plurality and post-2015 politics remain open research tasks. The case is not a score for Tanzania, Tanzanians, Tanganyika, Zanzibar, CCM, TANU, any party, region, ethnicity, language, religion or current government.',
