  claim: 'Vandewalle and St John provide independent long-run histories of colonialism, decolonization, the Sanusi monarchy, Qaddafi’s revolutionary state and the post-2011 crisis. Pargeter’s focused regime history helps interpret Qaddafi’s personalized authority and ideological claims, while Lacher’s fieldwork-based study documents how localistic military mobilization, bargaining and external support fragmented the post-2011 political order. Pargeter’s later comparative book adds a cautious account of tribal actors and state brokerage without treating “tribal” as an exhaustive Libyan identity; Haimzadeh’s French-language field account supplies an additional on-the-ground perspective on the Qaddafi era and 2011 opening. The 1951 constitutional record anchors an independence-era representative monarchy; the 2011 Constitutional Declaration records a temporary post-revolutionary framework and Constituent Assembly project; the 2015 Libyan Political Agreement formally proposed a House of Representatives, Government of National Accord, State Council, separation of powers and a state monopoly of force. On the six-axis framework, the bounded evidence supports a mixed and changing profile: constitutional and rights language alongside monarchical and revolutionary state-building, anti-colonial sovereignty, Qaddafi-era personalized command, oil-rentier political economy, contested religious–civic settlement, and post-2011 fragmentation. These are institutional and historical interpretations, not numerical scores or claims that one Libyan current represents the country.',
