    claim: 'Wyatt and Baker–Phongpaichit provide independent broad histories of Thailand’s state formation, social change and political conflict; Batson supplies a focused book-length study of the end of absolute monarchy. The French institutional witness records the 27 June provisional and 10 December permanent constitutional sequence, while Sugiyama and Fuwongcharoen interpret the People’s Party transition, its disputed social meaning and the later effort to mobilize society around the constitution. The bounded evidence supports describing 1932 as a revolutionary constitutional-monarchical transition: royal absolutism was displaced, a representative and legal framework was introduced, and authority remained contested rather than becoming an already consolidated liberal democracy.',
