    claim: 'Campbell’s account of Tonga’s modern political history and his book-length study of the road to democracy provide historical perspectives on the 2010 settlement; Powles and Tupou offer attributed constitutional-reform analyses, with Tupou emphasizing partial devolution, power-sharing, constitutional realism and talanoa. The Electoral Commission’s results portal supplies the institutional procedure for constituency counting, while Parliament records the 2023–2024 no-confidence, judicial-review, resignation and prime-minister-designate sequence. On the six-axis framework, the bounded evidence supports a mixed and changing interpretation: a small-island political economy shaped by public administration, aid, remittances, tourism and development questions rather than a simple market or command model; formal constitutional citizenship and electoral participation alongside unresolved evidence about rank, gender, island, diaspora and socioeconomic access; a monarchy retaining reserved powers alongside elected cabinets, the Legislative Assembly, no-confidence politics and judicial review; Tongan national, kinship, chiefly, island and diaspora identities interacting with Christian and customary institutions; Pacific regional, aid, climate and disaster contexts without a complete foreign-policy history; and Christian political history without evidence of clerical government. No numerical scores or claims about all Tongans are inferred.',
    limitation: 'A bounded Tonga case from the 2010 reform through the 2024 parliamentary leadership transition, not a current-country claim or a score for Tongans, nobles, commoners, women, men, youth, islands, diaspora, churches, parties, voters, the monarchy, Parliament, the Supreme Court or any later government. Official electoral and parliamentary records document formal procedures and institutional events; they do not by themselves establish equal participation, popular consent, local implementation, judicial independence, political economy, gender or land outcomes, or the views of all communities. The book coverage is primarily English-language, and Tongan-language and local oral histories, complete election returns, parliamentary debates, gender, land, outer-island and diaspora politics, local government, climate and disaster governance, socioeconomic effects and post-2024 developments remain open research tasks. No later current status is asserted.',
